---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Developer docs"
  description="Install and use the Texuli emoji catalog and engine. Includes API examples and migration notes for universal-emoji-parser."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Developer docs</p>
  <h1>Use Unicode emoji in your app</h1>
  <p class="lead">EmojiParser.com is an independent product built on the Texuli Emojis catalog and engine.</p>
  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Install</h2>
    <p><code>@texuli/emojis</code> is currently private and unpublished. Inside the Texuli workspace, depend on it with <code>"@texuli/emojis": "workspace:*"</code>. Public npm installation instructions will be added after an approved release.</p>
    <pre><code>import {'{'} findEmojis, toShortcodes, toUnicode {'}'} from '@texuli/emojis';
import {'{'} searchEmojis {'}'} from '@texuli/emojis/search';</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section class="panel">
    <h2>API</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>findEmojis(text)</code> returns longest matches with stable IDs and UTF-16 offsets.</li>
      <li><code>toShortcodes(text)</code> writes Texuli canonical shortcodes.</li>
      <li><code>toUnicode(text)</code> reads Texuli canonical shortcodes.</li>
      <li><code>searchEmojis(query)</code> searches CLDR names, aliases and keywords.</li>
      <li><code>toUnicodeLegacy(text, {'{'} mode: 'strict' {'}'})</code> reads the separate legacy alias dialect and returns conflict and unknown diagnostics.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Legacy chat aliases such as <code>:thumbsup:</code> and <code>:+1:</code> are opt-in. The default shortcode is <code>:thumbs_up:</code>.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Migrate from universal-emoji-parser</h2>
    <p>The migration guide explains canonical and legacy imports, escaped text handling, and intentional behavior differences.</p>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/DailybotHQ/texuli/blob/main/docs/emojis/MIGRATION.md">Read the universal-emoji-parser migration guide</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Relationship to Texuli</h2>
    <p>EmojiParser.com has its own product identity and roadmap. It uses the open-source Texuli Emojis package for catalog, search and conversion behavior; it does not keep a second emoji list or parsing engine.</p>
    <p><a href="/licensing/">Artwork and data licensing</a></p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
